#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Claude Desktop / Claude Code provider manager.

This is the Claude branch of the unified Codex & Claude Code model switcher.
It manages the user-level Claude Code persistent configuration
(``~/.claude/settings.json``), which is honored both by a standalone
``claude`` CLI and by the Claude Code engine bundled inside the Claude
Desktop app (the Desktop passes ``setting-sources=user`` to that engine).

DeepSeek connectivity uses DeepSeek's native Anthropic-compatible endpoint
(``https://api.deepseek.com/anthropic``) directly — never the Codex proxy.

Two configuration layers are managed together so the switch actually takes
effect for the Claude Desktop app (the primary target):

1. ``~/.claude/settings.json`` — official user config for the Claude Code
   engine (env + apiKeyHelper).  Also covers a standalone ``claude`` CLI.
2. ``~/Library/Application Support/Claude/developer_settings.json`` — the
   Claude Desktop "Developer Mode" / Custom 3p provider config.  In DeepSeek
   mode the Desktop provider becomes a gateway pointing at DeepSeek's
   Anthropic-compatible endpoint, with a credential helper backed by the
   macOS Keychain and a model list whose entries carry Anthropic family
   tiers (opus -> DeepSeek V4 Pro, sonnet/haiku -> DeepSeek V4 Flash).  The
   switcher reorders the list so the requested mode is the default entry.
   The Desktop reads this file at app start, which is why the GUI restarts
   Claude Desktop after a switch (like it does for Codex).

Model mapping follows DeepSeek's official Claude mapping: requests that carry
``claude-opus*`` model ids resolve to DeepSeek V4 Pro, and ``claude-sonnet*`` /
``claude-haiku*`` ids resolve to DeepSeek V4 Flash.  We therefore configure
canonical Anthropic model ids (``claude-opus-5`` / ``claude-sonnet-5``) and
let DeepSeek do the mapping server-side.

Security rules:

* The DeepSeek API key lives only in the macOS Keychain under the service
  ``claude-code-deepseek-api-key`` (independent of the Codex service).
* ``settings.json`` never contains the key.  Instead the official Claude Code
  ``apiKeyHelper`` setting runs ``/usr/bin/security ... -w`` at request time.
* Only the ``env`` object and ``apiKeyHelper`` are touched; permissions,
  hooks, MCP servers, plugins, projects and everything else are preserved.
* Writes are atomic; every switch is backed up first; failures roll back;
  a lock prevents concurrent switches; the result is verified after writing.
* When switching to DeepSeek, the current macOS HTTP/HTTPS system proxy
  (``/usr/sbin/scutil --proxy``, same parsing design as the Codex bridge)
  is written dynamically into ``env.HTTP_PROXY`` / ``env.HTTPS_PROXY`` so
  the engine can reach ``api.deepseek.com``.  If no system proxy is
  configured, no invalid proxy values are generated and any pre-existing
  user values are kept.  The proxy keys are part of the managed env set,
  so "Claude 官方" restores the user's original pre-switch values.
"""

